 In this module we'll learn how Python lets you wrap extra behavior around a function without changing the function itself — the decorator pattern for reusable concerns like timing, logging, retrying operations that fail, and caching results so they aren't recomputed. We'll cover decorators that take their own arguments and how stacking multiple decorators composes their effects. We'll also learn how to annotate variables, parameters, and return values with type hints — including the functions we pass around — so editors can catch type mismatches before the code runs. We'll put this together into a reusable toolkit of decorators that we apply to real functions and measure.

